      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "This is the first study reporting the application of Enhanced Recovery Principles (ERP) to revision arthroplasty."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Retrospective series of 132 revision hip and knee replacements treated with ERP."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Infiltration was associated with reduced LOS in knees (6 vs 8.5 days), lower PCA usage and incidence of transfusion in knees (2 vs 3 days) and hips (1 vs 6 days). Revisions for infection had a longer LOS (5.4 vs 11.5 days p = 0.001), a greater use of PCA and a higher incidence of transfusion (5 vs 0) in both knees and hips."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "The application of ERPs to revision arthroplasty is safe. Infiltration appears to be an important factor in improving outcome measures."
        }
      ]},
